  "articleBody": "Getting your electrical rough-in done right is a critical step in any remodeling project. Here's how to ensure it goes smoothly: First, you'll want to overlay those electrical plans with any other trade drawings, like plumbing or HVAC, as early as possible. This helps catch potential conflicts before they become problems. Next, make sure you confirm all routing and device locations – think outlets, lights, switches – in a preconstruction meeting. Everyone needs to be on the same page! It's also super important to coordinate with all the downstream trades *before* walls get closed up. This prevents surprises and ensures everything is accessible. Finally, before any inspections or closing in, verify all final clearances and access points. Electrical work usually happens after framing but before walls are closed, and inspections are typically required before anything gets concealed. Building in permit and inspection timing into your schedule is key. When your project involves service upgrades, multi-trade rough-ins, or tight inspection deadlines, bringing in an experienced professional like HH Remodeling LLC can save you a lot of hassle and make sure the job is done correctly and safely.",
  "datePublished": "2026-08-09"
}